build/
target/
# boards/<slug>/ IS tracked — definition, not product. The manifest, the ideas,
# the board README and lane-1.md are input; `boards/<slug>/work/` is what a run
# BUILDS, and it is deliberately NOT tracked: a board's output belongs to the
# TARGET project, which has a repository of its own (blade-workspace, say) and
# is not part of this kanban project. Nothing stages under work/, so no earlier
# version of a deliverable is retrievable here and a card has no history to dig
# into; the work dir is read on disk and by path, and a card reports
# `GIT ABSENT — nothing staged` instead (worker contract, GIT IS OPTIONAL).
#
# An installed dependency tree (node_modules/) and tool caches never belong in
# work/ at all: node_modules/ is reproducible from a lockfile, and a cache is not a
# deliverable. The audit NOTES one it finds in work/ (E16) and leaves it in place —
# a note, never a failure, because the board deletes nothing.
node_modules/
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
#
# `boards/<slug>/runs/` is per-run state plus the intermediate hand-offs (the
# refined idea, the plan, per-card patches, timing, the document chain): never
# staged, never committed — hand-offs travel by path, not through git, and a
# staged one reaches every later card's `git diff --cached` (run-audit E14).
boards/*/runs/
boards/*/work/

# Verdict texts captured by hand from real runs; nothing reads them.
tests/integration/verdicts/

# Claude Code: settings.json is the repo's shared allow-list; the local file is
# one person's.
.claude/settings.local.json

# IDE
.idea/
.classpath
.project
.settings/
